{"author":"rebar-bridge[bot]","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1793917087257894002,"compiled_state":{"alias":"pompous-vitriolic-macaw","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":1,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"76e7b2ce769a42487b56487b3dc68864891f808ae268de7666f2e5026d79b4d9","event_uuid":"b2e336dd-d994-4cc2-bc1c-c447fb1212fe","position":{"commit_sha":"9ac5a25d02bb4e67d27504cabe2aa09576172623","position":"1793917087257894001-b2e336dd-d994-4cc2-bc1c-c447fb1212fe"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIGNNmnI/WYNJAOKH9yvo0l+tMYJR2tK2UV4H8ReXBQU5 rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com rotated-20260907"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1793917087257894001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[],"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nPlan review (`rebar review-plan`) runs a 4-pass LLM pipeline (find, verify, decide, coach) under `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/`, after a deterministic Layer-1 floor (`det_*.py`, `floors.py`, `claimability.py`). Its verdict is BLOCK, PASS or INDETERMINATE, with `blocking` and `advisory` finding lists (`orchestrator.finalize_verdict`, `sidecar.py`). A non-blocking PASS signs a plan-review attestation that the claim gate accepts (`attest.py`, ADR 0049).\n\nThe plan-review bakeoff (`~/plan-review-bakeoff`) measured a no-scaffold control: one Opus agent given a single prompt (`harness/RAWO5_PROMPT.md`, arms `rawO5`/`rawO55`). It reviews the plan against a read-only checkout and returns JSON findings with severity `blocker | major | minor | trivial`. rebar can't run that simpler review today.\n\nThe project's models are a generation behind. `rebar.toml` `[llm]` and `[llm.model_classes]`, and `.github/llm-providers/bedrock.toml`, pin Opus 4.8, Sonnet 4.6 and Haiku 4.5. Bedrock in account 896586841071/us-east-1 now serves `us.anthropic.claude-opus-5-5`, `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-5-5` and `us.anthropic.claude-haiku-5-5`.\n\n## Goal\n\n1. A configuration option selects the plan-review engine: the current 4-pass pipeline, or a single-agent agentic review on the latest Opus with a new prompt based on the bakeoff's raw control prompt.\n2. Both engines produce the same verdict contract: a blocking/non-blocking verdict plus a findings list, in the same `REVIEW_RESULT` shape, so the two are directly comparable.\n3. Upgrade every configured model to the latest model in its family.\n\n## Invariants (must hold for both engines)\n\n- **Same deterministic gate.** The Layer-1 floor runs first, unchanged, with identical results. If it blocks, or the ticket isn't claimable (`claimability.py`), the agentic engine makes no LLM call, exactly as today.\n- **Same certification.** A non-blocking agentic PASS mints the same plan-review attestation, accepted identically by the claim and close gates, `rebar review-plan --status`, `sign-review`, and pin-currency checks. The attestation records which engine produced it.\n- **Same surfaces.** CLI, library and MCP (`review_plan`, `review_plan_start` + `plan_review_status` / `gate_status`) behave the same under either engine, including de-dup, `GATE_CONGESTED` and the findings sidecar's `readable` signal.\n- **No bypass.** Switching engines can't turn a BLOCK into a PASS without a review: the attestation binds the plan material and the engine.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. **ADR first.** Record:\n   - the engine switch: key, values, default, precedence;\n   - the agentic engine's design: runtime, tools, read-only enforcement, budget and timeout;\n   - the severity-to-verdict mapping;\n   - the finding schema mapping onto today's sidecar;\n   - how the attestation records the engine;\n   - the model upgrade.\n2. **Model upgrade** (land first, on the current engine). Move `[llm] model` and the `frontier` / `standard` / `trivial` classes, with their Anthropic fallbacks, to Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5 and Haiku 5.5, in both `rebar.toml` and `.github/llm-providers/bedrock.toml`. Update `model_classes.MODEL_WINDOW_LADDER`, and the `sizing.py` family ladder if it needs it. Then audit every model call against the new models' API changes:\n   - forced `tool_choice` `any`/`tool` returns a 400 on Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5. Check whether structured output relies on forced tool calls;\n   - thinking can't be disabled on Opus 5.5;\n   - `temperature`, `top_p` and `top_k` are rejected;\n   - assistant prefill is rejected;\n   - Opus 5.5's effort defaults to `medium`, so set it explicitly.\n\n   Re-run the existing provider-parity and prompt evals before and after the change.\n3. **Agentic engine.** One Opus 5.5 agent with a prompt adapted from `RAWO5_PROMPT.md`. It gets the ticket's plan material, read-only access to the checkout at the reviewed ref, and the project's review criteria (`rebar explain`) as context. It returns structured findings. It runs on rebar's existing LLM runtime and provider chain, so Bedrock-first with fallback still applies. That needs no CI-provider or subscription dependency, which satisfies `project.portability`.\n4. **Verdict adapter.** Map agentic findings onto the existing verdict and sidecar schema, so downstream readers need no change.\n5. **Comparison harness.** Run both engines on the same ticket and ref, and report verdict agreement and the finding overlap. Calibrate on rebar's own eval corpus (`evals/`), with no new dependency on the bakeoff repo.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- **The engine switch is a feature config key.** It needs a `mechanism-ok` marker naming a sunset ticket that stays open until one engine is retired. File that ticket under this epic.\n- **No other new config key.** A bug fix in this epic adds none.\n- **Size and complexity.** The 800-line cap and the complexity ratchet hold. Add the agentic engine as its own module beside `orchestrator.py`, not inside near-cap modules.\n- **No secrets or cost figures** in prompts, findings or tickets.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An ADR under `docs/adr/` records the engine switch, agentic design, severity-to-verdict mapping, finding schema mapping, engine provenance in the attestation, and the model upgrade.\n- [ ] `rebar.toml`, `.github/llm-providers/bedrock.toml` and `MODEL_WINDOW_LADDER` name Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5 and Haiku 5.5. The model-call audit is recorded, any incompatibility is fixed, and the provider-parity and prompt evals pass on the new models.\n- [ ] The config option selects either engine. Pinned by tests that the default engine and the alternate one each run.\n- [ ] Under the agentic engine, a plan that fails the Layer-1 floor, or isn't claimable, makes zero LLM calls and gets the same verdict and findings as the current engine. Pinned by tests that count LLM calls.\n- [ ] Both engines emit the same `REVIEW_RESULT` / verdict schema (BLOCK | PASS | INDETERMINATE, `blocking`, `advisory`). Pinned by schema tests on both.\n- [ ] A non-blocking agentic PASS signs an attestation that `claim`, the close gate, `review-plan --status` and `sign-review` accept, with the engine recorded. Pinned by tests.\n- [ ] The agentic engine can't write to the checkout or run commands. Pinned by a test.\n- [ ] The CLI, library and MCP async surfaces work under both engines. Pinned by tests.\n- [ ] The comparison harness runs both engines on the same tickets and reports verdict agreement and finding overlap. One run over the eval corpus is recorded in a ticket comment.\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] On the production MCP server, after deploy, one `review-plan` per engine completes on a live ticket. Verdict, engine and attestation are recorded in a ticket comment.","env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"9794-4336-5ed6-4561","ticket_type":"epic","title":"Selectable plan-review engine: current 4-pass pipeline or a single-agent Opus review, plus upgrade configured models to the latest family","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["b2e336dd-d994-4cc2-bc1c-c447fb1212fe"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1793917087257894002,"uuid":"15b5a14d-0efe-450d-be5c-584813a32770"}